Yes. You’re describing a constructed third point used as a temporary coordinate—not a third measurement pretending to be real.

Start with two grounded signals:

A,\;B

Their relation gives a delta:

\Delta_{AB}=B-A

Now construct an imaginary third point C^* from them:

C^*=f(A,B,\Delta_{AB})

C^* could be a midpoint, extrapolation, inversion, ideal state, counterfactual, predicted continuation, or entirely different perspective. Its value is that it gives the pair geometry.

Instead of:

A\leftrightarrow B

you now have:

\triangle(A,B,C^*)

The triangle is partly grounded and partly hypothetical. That’s okay as long as the imaginary vertex remains tagged as imaginary.

Then I think your “other ratios” move is where this gets interesting.

Take another pair:

D,E\rightarrow F^*

Now you have two constructed relational objects:

T_1=(A,B,C^*)

T_2=(D,E,F^*)

Rather than comparing the raw objects, compare their internal ratios:

R_1=
\left(
\frac{AB}{AC^*},
\frac{BC^*}{AB},
\ldots
\right)

R_2=
\left(
\frac{DE}{DF^*},
\frac{EF^*}{DE},
\ldots
\right)

If similar relational proportions survive across completely different domains, you’ve potentially discovered a higher-order pattern:

R_1\approx R_2
\Rightarrow
\boxed{\text{shared relational invariant}}

And then compress those multiple relations into one new object:

T_1\oplus T_2\oplus T_3
\rightarrow
G

So you’re climbing:

\text{points}
\rightarrow
\text{pairs}
\rightarrow
\text{constructed triangles}
\rightarrow
\text{ratios}
\rightarrow
\text{ratios between ratios}
\rightarrow
\text{higher-order object}

That’s a genuine representational strategy.

The imaginary point becomes scaffolding

And here’s the beautiful part: once G has been constructed, you might not need C^* anymore.

It behaved like scaffolding.

A+B
\rightarrow C^*
\rightarrow R
\rightarrow G

then:

C^* \;\text{can disappear}

while the relational structure it helped expose remains.

That’s extremely similar to imaginary constructs elsewhere in reasoning: introduce something that makes the transformation tractable, perform operations in the expanded space, then return with a result expressed in the original space.

This also gives the Origami Accordion another fold

I’d represent the operation compactly as:

\boxed{\triangle^*}

meaning:

Given two grounded points, generate a clearly tagged hypothetical third point that maximizes useful relational information; use the resulting triangle to extract ratios/invariants; then test whether the hypothetical scaffold can be removed while preserving the discovered structure.

Then composition becomes tiny:

(A,B)\xrightarrow{\triangle^*}T_1

(D,E)\xrightarrow{\triangle^*}T_2

T_1\;\Delta\;T_2\xrightarrow{\equiv}G

That’s precisely the sort of layer-scaled symbol we were looking for.

And there’s an important discipline built into it:

Imaginary points generate hypotheses. Grounded points adjudicate them.

Otherwise it’s easy to construct three imaginary points, discover a beautiful ratio among them, and mistake internally generated consistency for external evidence.

Used correctly, though, the imaginary third point becomes a temporary extra dimension for thought.

Two anchors.

One invented vertex.

Extract the geometry.

Compare geometries.

Collapse them into a higher-order invariant.

Then fold the scaffolding away.